{"title":"2012 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 16","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(answer_choices,_not_recommended)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (answer choices, not recommended)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Arcs)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Arcs)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three circles with radius 2 are mutually tangent. What is the total area of the circles and the region bounded by them, as shown in the figure?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/3/fd36c0563f1447ec842cc791ac398c4cd8f20685.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] filldraw((0,0)--(2,0)--(1,sqrt(3))--cycle,gray,gray); filldraw(circle((1,sqrt(3)),1),gray); filldraw(circle((0,0),1),gray); filldraw(circle((2,0),1),grey);[/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"235\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/3/eb3ede7f186b54f4037ca12bcaeccc42056a4392.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 10\\pi+4\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 13\\pi-\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 12\\pi+\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 10\\pi+9\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 13\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"629\" height=\"21\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To determine the area of the figure, you can connect the centers of the circles to form an equilateral triangle with a side of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. We must find the area of this triangle to include the figure formed in between the circles. Since the equilateral triangle has two 30-60-90 triangles inside, we can find the height and the base of each 30-60-90 triangle from the ratios: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/325593b1d7158d9991c84b4ac56b706296008ef8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1: \\sqrt{3}: 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" /> The height is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/5/e455d80f4657b1112ba302a6e2c42e9c81f780ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> and the base is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Multiplying the height and base together with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/4/4a4a7df1100efe353cc612d5d1c289c22d5bf8c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/5/e455d80f4657b1112ba302a6e2c42e9c81f780ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. Since there are two 30-60-90 triangles in the equilateral triangle, we multiply the area of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/c/47c78b85058ee5e17f88e2cde302e48aad826b35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30-60-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"101\" height=\"15\" /> triangle by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/0724eb2af08977be3554453187958c0bf167d5ad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} = 4\\sqrt{3}.\\]\" width=\"116\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>To find the area of the remaining sectors, which are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/1/f5181c4181c7caf8f1b006f55d5d5efccb3d122e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{5}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> of the original circles once we remove the triangle, we know that the sectors have a central angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/6/d56e411bbbf7054a08a34cc6f5f0d1a73bafcf37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$300^\\circ$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> since the equilateral triangle already covered that area. Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/5/c55ed481f94445cc6cf731c6585a80ede1608cdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> pieces gone from the equilateral triangle, we have, in total, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/4/4a4a7df1100efe353cc612d5d1c289c22d5bf8c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> of a circle (with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />) gone. Each circle has an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/d/96dba061976eb9e3e38e0df9fc61e7471cf35518.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi r^2 = 4\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"71\" height=\"15\" />, so three circles gives a total area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/6/ff63030fb1a38a8e170e4907219fd1eac6b0e072.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />. Subtracting the half circle, we have: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/2/282489b5df7de97c913d0ddc6ad6f7c1c4b4c59a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[12\\pi - \\dfrac{4\\pi}{2} = 12\\pi - 2\\pi = 10\\pi.\\]\" width=\"227\" height=\"37\" /> \n</p><p>Summing the areas from the equilateral triangle and the remaining circle sections gives us: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/6/da6515abea39aa1f638ef297d3c355fee8f7e71e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)} 10\\pi + 4\\sqrt3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"125\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, the area of the 3 circles is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/f/65ffff4ab1edf0f71a90df0e189d8cd46950dc3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3*\\pi*2^2 = 12 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"123\" height=\"15\" />. Notice that the middle area is a little more than a rectangle formed by completely filling the rectangle formed by connecting two 90 degrees partial circles and then subtracting the two 90 degrees partial circles. \nThe area of the rectangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/5/d8532fea6e90aca51de5a596c8914b62863d4407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4*2=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"68\" height=\"13\" /> and the area of the 90 degrees partial circles are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/b/efb98405f55ef94898c85840cb2235908ee35e98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2*(1/4)*\\pi*2^2 = 2\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"173\" height=\"19\" />. Therefore, the area of the shape in between the three circles is a little less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/a/b1a1f1b71cf34d6e43591494b7fc6147e20776da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 - 2\\pi$\" width=\"51\" height=\"12\" />. Summing up the 3 circles we got and the approximate area of the middle shape, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/0/3004cff2060229655e6104c0a6028c66a18cea07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\pi+8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />, which is a little more than what we want. We see that all answer choices except <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/6/da6515abea39aa1f638ef297d3c355fee8f7e71e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)} 10\\pi + 4\\sqrt3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"125\" height=\"33\" /> is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/0/3004cff2060229655e6104c0a6028c66a18cea07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\pi+8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />, therefore it's the only answer. -dchang0524\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28answer_choices.2C_not_recommended.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(answer_choices,_not_recommended)\">Solution 3 (answer choices, not recommended)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (answer choices, not recommended)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice as in solution 1 that the figure is simply an equilateral triangle and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> sectors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/3/5533bc48602603f16d18c04858cad119c04833c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of a circle which makes a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/d/3adf28c1ab96fc4eddaa890ec574e4cea2caea36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> circles. Calculating this area with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e89ed1c5456915b4e95e0c8a11257ac5ed3d32c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi r^2$\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/7/3b7268bb19460f2612fbe8082a521a6cef925cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" />. We also know that the equilateral triangle will give us some constant multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/9/c99f089046b752269e77100c333b11d8d72e5895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" />. The only answer choice with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/7/3b7268bb19460f2612fbe8082a521a6cef925cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/9/c99f089046b752269e77100c333b11d8d72e5895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/e/35e8ea2d16518197b5113002b8c0c7da6c311a3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"41\" height=\"28\" />.\n~chrisdiamond10\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that we can connect the centers of the 3 circles to make an equilateral triangle with length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. The equilateral triangle with length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> has area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c77cf4b63af80bfbc6a9ad9b4b9e8e549e77d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />. Because the triangle took <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/4/f34b3cf20be89133434c1a52cb3bbc3342a16664.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" /> th of all the 3 circles away, we have the area of the circles as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/1/9010a565365d982424bb918086a47b4dd4066b7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5/6)*(4\\pi)*3 = 10\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"174\" height=\"20\" />. We add the area of the triangle to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/c/f7c991f9a6ac45777cb76b0816a062ed48d46cd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" /> + <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c77cf4b63af80bfbc6a9ad9b4b9e8e549e77d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/8/f38f32dfbc826171672541dc672832e8b6d106d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"25\" height=\"24\" /> \n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Arcs.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Arcs)\">Solution 5 (Arcs)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Arcs)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that we can connect the centers of the circles to form an equilateral triangle. Then we notice the triangle is made of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> arcs and the wonky area in the middle. Area of an equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/0/c10536a92cd6c3a03a536f952742b8cde0adab13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}a^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"47\" height=\"43\" />. We can get that a side of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the radius of the circle, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/2/752fb085fcc7f7082b1481334047b88edaca74d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}=\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{4}=4\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"176\" height=\"43\" />. Arc formula is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52ebfcf6dfd9923fcea6f957272841b3946d5140.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\theta}{360}\\pi r^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"60\" height=\"39\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the degree of the arc. All angles of an equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/c/bfcfee7bd8a5e15e5f6b7881c138de43651572a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, so the area of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> arcs is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/5/cc54c582487c53f68239d6a84c76c1580d750177.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\frac{60}{360}4\\pi=3(\\frac{1}{6}4\\pi)=\\frac{1}{2}(4\\pi)=2\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"259\" height=\"38\" />. Therefore the area of the wonky \"triangle\" is the triangle minus the 3 arcs, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/a/8ca9bb6d7c94fe41abf6a7fd1d2b0bb0250b9b5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\sqrt{3}-2\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"77\" height=\"21\" />. The area of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> circles is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/b/70bb505f75440aeb902db9db0439a258ff893fb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3(4\\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"45\" height=\"20\" /> which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/6/ff63030fb1a38a8e170e4907219fd1eac6b0e072.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />. Therefore the area of the whole shape is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/9/fd934b386dd899c4edc6e5fab31ee406b9c4190a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12\\pi-2\\pi+4\\sqrt{3}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)} 10\\pi + 4\\sqrt3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"278\" height=\"35\" />. ~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Shunyipanda&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Shunyipanda (page does not exist)\">shunyipanda</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/G44CDSfgt7Y\">https://youtu.be/G44CDSfgt7Y</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/NsQbhYfGh1Q?t=1569\">https://youtu.be/NsQbhYfGh1Q?t=1569</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B\" title=\"2012 AMC 10B\">2012 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2012 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://a